/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 26-30
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:26, name:"The Song of Perfect Peace", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 26:3",verse:"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee."}, facts:[
"Did you know Isaiah 26 is actually a song? It opens with, In that day shall this song be sung in the land of Judah, showing that God's people answer coming judgment with praise, not panic.",
"Did you know Isaiah 26:4 joins two of God's names together, the LORD JEHOVAH? It is one of the rare verses where both names appear side by side, marking Him as the God of everlasting strength.",
"Did you know Isaiah 26:19 contains one of the clearest Old Testament declarations of bodily resurrection? Awake and sing, ye that dwell in dust, spoken centuries before the New Testament."
], questions:[
{q:"In Isaiah 26:1, what does God appoint as walls and bulwarks for the strong city?", options:["Salvation","The armies of Judah","Gates of bronze","The walls of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:1",verse:"In that day shall this song be sung in the land of Judah; We have a strong city; salvation will God appoint for walls and bulwarks.", insight:"God's own salvation is the true defense of His people. Stronger than any wall is the safety He gives to those who trust Him."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:2, who may enter through the opened gates?", options:["The righteous nation which keepeth the truth","The merchants of the city","The warriors with swords","The singers of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:2",verse:"Open ye the gates, that the righteous nation which keepeth the truth may enter in.", insight:"The gates open not to the powerful or the wealthy but to the righteous who keep the truth. Character, not status, is the entrance requirement."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:3, whom will God keep in perfect peace?", options:["Him whose mind is stayed on thee","Him whose hands are strongest","Him whose wealth is greatest","Him whose name is honored"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:3",verse:"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee.", insight:"Perfect peace is not the absence of trouble but the presence of a steady mind fixed on God. Where our thoughts rest determines whether our hearts rest."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:4, why should we trust in the LORD forever?", options:["For in the LORD JEHOVAH is everlasting strength","For He rewards the rich with more","For He never asks for obedience","For He answers every prayer at once"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:4",verse:"Trust ye in the LORD for ever: for in the LORD JEHOVAH is everlasting strength:", insight:"Everlasting strength belongs to God alone, which is why trust in Him can be forever. Anything less than eternal would fail us sooner or later."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:5, what does God do to the lofty city?", options:["He layeth it low, even to the dust","He sets it on a higher hill","He fills it with gold","He gives it to the merchants"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:5",verse:"For he bringeth down them that dwell on high; the lofty city, he layeth it low; he layeth it low, even to the ground; he bringeth it even to the dust.", insight:"Pride builds high, but God brings the lofty low. Whatever rises against Him in arrogance ends in the dust."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:6, whose feet shall tread down the lofty city?", options:["The feet of the poor, and the steps of the needy","The feet of the king's army","The feet of the temple guards","The feet of the trading caravans"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:6",verse:"The foot shall tread it down, even the feet of the poor, and the steps of the needy.", insight:"God delights in reversing the world's order. The poor and needy, so often trampled, become the ones who tread down the proud city."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:7, what is the way of the just?", options:["Uprightness","Wealth","Silence","Speed"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:7",verse:"The way of the just is uprightness: thou, most upright, dost weigh the path of the just.", insight:"The just walk a straight path, and God Himself weighs it. Uprightness is not just the right direction, it is the smoothest road."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:8, what is the desire of our soul?", options:["Thy name, and the remembrance of thee","Riches and long life","Victory over our enemies","The harvest of the fields"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:8",verse:"Yea, in the way of thy judgments, O LORD, have we waited for thee; the desire of our soul is to thy name, and to the remembrance of thee.", insight:"Even while waiting on God's judgments, the singer's deepest want is God Himself. His name and remembrance are the true treasure."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:9, when do the inhabitants of the world learn righteousness?", options:["When thy judgments are in the earth","When their crops are plentiful","When their kings are kind","When their borders are safe"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:9",verse:"With my soul have I desired thee in the night; yea, with my spirit within me will I seek thee early: for when thy judgments are in the earth, the inhabitants of the world will learn righteousness.", insight:"God's judgments are a classroom. The world learns righteousness not in ease but when God acts openly in the earth."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:10, even if favor is shown to the wicked, what will he not do?", options:["He will not learn righteousness","He will not accept the gift","He will not enter the city","He will not speak at all"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:10",verse:"Let favour be shewed to the wicked, yet will he not learn righteousness: in the land of uprightness will he deal unjustly, and will not behold the majesty of the LORD.", insight:"Kindness alone cannot change a stubborn heart. Without repentance, even favor is wasted on the wicked."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:11, what shall devour God's enemies?", options:["The fire of thine enemies","A flood of waters","A plague of locusts","The sword of the king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:11",verse:"LORD, when thy hand is lifted up, they will not see: but they shall see, and be ashamed for their envy at the people; yea, the fire of thine enemies shall devour them.", insight:"Those who envy God's people and ignore His lifted hand will face His fire. Refusing to see God's majesty does not make it disappear."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:12, who has wrought all our works in us?", options:["The LORD","Our own hands","The priests alone","The king of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:12",verse:"LORD, thou wilt ordain peace for us: for thou also hast wrought all our works in us.", insight:"Even our good works are God's doing in us. Peace comes from knowing that both the plan and the power are His."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:13, what besides God has had dominion over us?", options:["Other lords","Other nations' gods of wood","Our own fears","The merchants"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:13",verse:"O LORD our God, other lords besides thee have had dominion over us: but by thee only will we make mention of thy name.", insight:"Many masters have ruled God's people, but only God's name is worth mentioning. Remembering this frees us from every lesser lord."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:14, what is said of the dead oppressors?", options:["They shall not live, they shall not rise","They shall return in greater power","They shall be honored forever","They shall sleep for a little while"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:14",verse:"They are dead, they shall not live; they are deceased, they shall not rise: therefore hast thou visited and destroyed them, and made all their memory to perish.", insight:"God's judgment on His enemies is final. Their memory perishes, which is the comfort of all who suffered under them."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:15, what has God done to the nation?", options:["He has increased the nation","He has scattered the nation","He has taxed the nation","He has hidden the nation"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:15",verse:"Thou hast increased the nation, O LORD, thou hast increased the nation: thou art glorified: thou hadst removed it far unto all the ends of the earth.", insight:"God's discipline ends in growth, not ruin. He enlarges His people and is glorified in their increase."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:16, what did the people pour out when God's chastening was upon them?", options:["A prayer","Their gold","Their complaints","Their weapons"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:16",verse:"LORD, in trouble have they visited thee, they poured out a prayer when thy chastening was upon them.", insight:"Trouble drove them to visit God in prayer. Chastening fulfilled its purpose when it turned their hearts back to Him."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:17, to what are God's people compared in their trouble?", options:["A woman with child near the time of her delivery","A soldier wounded in battle","A farmer in a drought","A shepherd who lost his flock"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:17",verse:"Like as a woman with child, that draweth near the time of her delivery, is in pain, and crieth out in her pangs; so have we been in thy sight, O LORD.", insight:"Their pain before God was real and intense, like labor pains. God does not despise honest anguish poured out in His sight."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:18, what did they bring forth after all their pain?", options:["Wind","A mighty army","A great harvest","A new song"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:18",verse:"We have been with child, we have been in pain, we have as it were brought forth wind; we have not wrought any deliverance in the earth; neither have the inhabitants of the world fallen.", insight:"All their striving produced nothing, only wind. Human effort without God labors hard and delivers nothing."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:19, what shall happen to God's dead?", options:["They shall live, and arise from the dust","They shall sleep forever","They shall become as the wind","They shall be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:19",verse:"Thy dead men shall live, together with my dead body shall they arise. Awake and sing, ye that dwell in dust: for thy dew is as the dew of herbs, and the earth shall cast out the dead.", insight:"God's answer to death is resurrection. The same voice that calls the sleeping to awake and sing will raise all who belong to Him."},
{q:"In Isaiah 26:20, what are God's people told to do until the indignation passes?", options:["Enter their chambers and shut their doors","Flee to the mountains","Gather weapons for battle","Sound the trumpet in the streets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 26:20",verse:"Come, my people, enter thou into thy chambers, and shut thy doors about thee: hide thyself as it were for a little moment, until the indignation be overpast.", insight:"Sometimes God's command is to hide, not fight. He shelters His people for a little moment while His judgment passes over."}
]},
{n:27, name:"The Vineyard the LORD Keeps", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 27:3",verse:"I the LORD do keep it; I will water it every moment: lest any hurt it, I will keep it night and day."}, facts:[
"Did you know the leviathan in Isaiah 27:1 is a picture of proud, chaotic powers? God's sore and great and strong sword shows that no monster, however mighty, can stand against Him.",
"Did you know Isaiah 27 turns the vineyard song of chapter 5 upside down? There the vineyard failed and was trampled, but here God Himself waters it every moment and keeps it night and day.",
"Did you know the chapter ends with the great trumpet? It gathers God's people from Assyria and Egypt to worship at Jerusalem, a picture of the final homecoming."
], questions:[
{q:"In Isaiah 27:1, with what will the LORD punish leviathan?", options:["His sore and great and strong sword","His bow of bronze","His chariot of fire","His rod of iron"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:1",verse:"In that day the LORD with his sore and great and strong sword shall punish leviathan the piercing serpent, even leviathan that crooked serpent; and he shall slay the dragon that is in the sea.", insight:"God meets the greatest chaos with His own great sword. No serpent, however piercing or crooked, is beyond His reach."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:1, what shall the LORD slay?", options:["The dragon that is in the sea","The lion of the desert","The bear of the forest","The eagle of the mountains"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:1",verse:"In that day the LORD with his sore and great and strong sword shall punish leviathan the piercing serpent, even leviathan that crooked serpent; and he shall slay the dragon that is in the sea.", insight:"The dragon in the sea stands for every deep, dark power. God will slay it, showing His people that evil's strongholds are not safe from Him."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:2, what are they told to sing about in that day?", options:["A vineyard of red wine","A city of strong walls","A river of clear water","A mountain of high cedars"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:2",verse:"In that day sing ye unto her, A vineyard of red wine.", insight:"After judgment comes a love song over the vineyard. God's final word over His people is not a woe but a song."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:3, how often will the LORD water His vineyard?", options:["Every moment","Once a year","When the rains come","At the morning sacrifice"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:3",verse:"I the LORD do keep it; I will water it every moment: lest any hurt it, I will keep it night and day.", insight:"God's care is constant, every moment, night and day. His people are never left unwatered or unwatched, not for a single breath."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:4, what does the LORD say is not in Him?", options:["Fury","Mercy","Justice","Wisdom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:4",verse:"Fury is not in me: who would set the briers and thorns against me in battle? I would go through them, I would burn them together.", insight:"God declares He has no fury toward His vineyard. His discipline is measured; His heart toward His own is tender, not raging."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:5, what may a person make with God by taking hold of His strength?", options:["Peace","War","Riches","A name"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:5",verse:"Or let him take hold of my strength, that he may make peace with me; and he shall make peace with me.", insight:"Even God's enemies are invited to take hold of His strength and make peace. Surrender to God is the only path that ends in peace."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:6, what shall Israel do?", options:["Blossom and bud, and fill the face of the world with fruit","Build walls around every city","Gather silver from every nation","Number the stars of heaven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:6",verse:"He shall cause them that come of Jacob to take root: Israel shall blossom and bud, and fill the face of the world with fruit.", insight:"God's plan for Israel was never small. Rooted by Him, they would blossom until the whole world tasted the fruit."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:6, who shall take root?", options:["They that come of Jacob","The kings of the earth","The merchants of Tyre","The elders of the gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:6",verse:"He shall cause them that come of Jacob to take root: Israel shall blossom and bud, and fill the face of the world with fruit.", insight:"God starts with a remnant, those who come of Jacob, and grows a worldwide harvest from it. Small beginnings are His specialty."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:7, did God smite Israel as He smote those who smote Israel?", options:["No, His stroke on Israel was measured and lighter","Yes, exactly the same","No, He never touched Israel at all","Yes, but only at night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:7",verse:"Hath he smitten him, as he smote those that smote him? or is he slain according to the slaughter of them that are slain by him?", insight:"God disciplines His own differently than He judges their enemies. His hand on His people is a father's correction, not a conqueror's slaughter."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:8, what does God stay in the day of the east wind?", options:["His rough wind","His gentle rain","His morning dew","His evening shade"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:8",verse:"In measure, when it shooteth forth, thou wilt debate with it: he stayeth his rough wind in the day of the east wind.", insight:"Even God's rough wind is restrained. He measures His discipline, debating with His people in measure rather than destroying them outright."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:9, by what shall the iniquity of Jacob be purged?", options:["Making the altar stones as chalkstones beaten in sunder","Offering a thousand rams","Building a higher temple","Fasting for forty days"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:9",verse:"By this therefore shall the iniquity of Jacob be purged; and this is all the fruit to take away his sin; when he maketh all the stones of the altar as chalkstones that are beaten in sunder, the groves and images shall not stand up.", insight:"Purging begins with smashing idolatry to powder. God removes the altars, groves, and images before He removes the guilt."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:9, when the altar stones are broken, what shall not stand up?", options:["The groves and images","The city walls","The king's palace","The gates of brass"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:9",verse:"By this therefore shall the iniquity of Jacob be purged; and this is all the fruit to take away his sin; when he maketh all the stones of the altar as chalkstones that are beaten in sunder, the groves and images shall not stand up.", insight:"True repentance leaves the idols in ruins. Where God cleanses, the groves and images cannot stand."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:10, what shall feed in the desolate defenced city?", options:["The calf","The lion","The wild goat","The raven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:10",verse:"Yet the defenced city shall be desolate, and the habitation forsaken, and left like a wilderness: there shall the calf feed, and there shall he lie down, and consume the branches thereof.", insight:"A city that trusted its own defenses becomes a pasture for calves. Human strength, left godless, ends as an empty field."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:10, what will the defenced city become?", options:["Desolate, and left like a wilderness","Greater than before","A market for all nations","A dwelling for kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:10",verse:"Yet the defenced city shall be desolate, and the habitation forsaken, and left like a wilderness: there shall the calf feed, and there shall he lie down, and consume the branches thereof.", insight:"Defenses without understanding are no defense at all. The fortified city that forgot God is left as bare as a wilderness."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:11, why will God show the people no mercy?", options:["For it is a people of no understanding","For they were too poor","For they lived too far away","For they sang too loudly"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:11",verse:"When the boughs thereof are withered, they shall be broken off: the women come, and set them on fire: for it is a people of no understanding: therefore he that made them will not have mercy on them, and he that formed them will shew them no favour.", insight:"Refusing to understand is itself a judgment. A people that will not know God cannot expect His mercy while they persist in it."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:11, what will the women do with the withered boughs?", options:["Set them on fire","Weave them into baskets","Plant them by the river","Carry them to the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:11",verse:"When the boughs thereof are withered, they shall be broken off: the women come, and set them on fire: for it is a people of no understanding: therefore he that made them will not have mercy on them, and he that formed them will shew them no favour.", insight:"Withered branches are only fit for burning. Life disconnected from God dries up until nothing remains but fuel for the fire."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:12, from where to where will the LORD gather His people?", options:["From the channel of the river unto the stream of Egypt","From the east gate unto the west gate","From the mountains unto the sea","From the desert unto the forest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:12",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that the LORD shall beat off from the channel of the river unto the stream of Egypt, and ye shall be gathered one by one, O ye children of Israel.", insight:"God's harvest stretches from river to river, covering the lands of exile. No distance puts His scattered people beyond His reach."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:12, how shall the children of Israel be gathered?", options:["One by one","In chariots of fire","By the sound of thunder","On the wings of eagles"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:12",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that the LORD shall beat off from the channel of the river unto the stream of Egypt, and ye shall be gathered one by one, O ye children of Israel.", insight:"God gathers His people personally, one by one. His restoration is not a crowd swept along but each child sought and found."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:13, what shall be blown in that day?", options:["The great trumpet","The silver bell","The ram's horn of war","The pipe of the shepherd"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:13",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that the great trumpet shall be blown, and they shall come which were ready to perish in the land of Assyria, and the outcasts in the land of Egypt, and shall worship the LORD in the holy mount at Jerusalem.", insight:"The great trumpet is God's signal of homecoming. When He calls, even those ready to perish in Assyria will hear and come."},
{q:"In Isaiah 27:13, where shall the outcasts worship the LORD?", options:["In the holy mount at Jerusalem","In the plains of Moab","In the courts of Pharaoh","In the tents of Kedar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 27:13",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that the great trumpet shall be blown, and they shall come which were ready to perish in the land of Assyria, and the outcasts in the land of Egypt, and shall worship the LORD in the holy mount at Jerusalem.", insight:"The journey ends at the holy mount. Exiles from Assyria and Egypt find their way home to worship, which was God's purpose all along."}
]},
{n:28, name:"The Sure Cornerstone", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 28:16",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, I lay in Zion for a foundation a stone, a tried stone, a precious corner stone, a sure foundation: he that believeth shall not make haste."}, facts:[
"Did you know the cornerstone of Isaiah 28:16 is quoted in the New Testament of Jesus Christ? Romans and 1 Peter both point to Him as the tried stone, the precious cornerstone.",
"Did you know the phrase precept upon precept, line upon line became the classic picture of patient teaching? God gives His word a little at a time, the way a child learns to walk.",
"Did you know the end of this chapter is a farming parable? God is pictured as a wise husbandman who threshes each crop differently, because He disciplines each person exactly as needed."
], questions:[
{q:"In Isaiah 28:1, to whom is the woe pronounced?", options:["The drunkards of Ephraim","The merchants of Tyre","The shepherds of Moab","The singers of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:1",verse:"Woe to the crown of pride, to the drunkards of Ephraim, whose glorious beauty is a fading flower, which are on the head of the fat valleys of them that are overcome with wine!", insight:"The crown of pride sits on drunken heads in Ephraim. Glory built on indulgence is already fading before it falls."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:2, with what shall the Lord cast the proud down to the earth?", options:["A tempest of hail and a destroying storm","A gentle rain in spring","A soft wind at evening","A quiet snow in winter"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:2",verse:"Behold, the Lord hath a mighty and strong one, which as a tempest of hail and a destroying storm, as a flood of mighty waters overflowing, shall cast down to the earth with the hand.", insight:"God has His mighty one ready, like hail and overflowing flood. Human pride is no match for the forces He commands."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:3, what shall be trodden under feet?", options:["The crown of pride, the drunkards of Ephraim","The walls of the temple","The gates of the city","The altars of the groves"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:3",verse:"The crown of pride, the drunkards of Ephraim, shall be trodden under feet:", insight:"What was worn as a crown ends up underfoot. Pride's glory is temporary; its humiliation is certain."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:4, what shall the glorious beauty become like?", options:["A fading flower, and the hasty fruit before the summer","An evergreen tree by the river","A diamond that never dims","A star that never sets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:4",verse:"And the glorious beauty, which is on the head of the fat valley, shall be a fading flower, and as the hasty fruit before the summer; which when he that looketh upon it seeth, while it is yet in his hand he eateth it up.", insight:"Beauty without God fades fast, like an early fig snatched up and eaten. What is not rooted in Him cannot last."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:5, what shall the LORD of hosts be to the residue of His people?", options:["A crown of glory, and a diadem of beauty","A sword of vengeance","A wall of fire","A cloud of darkness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:5",verse:"In that day shall the LORD of hosts be for a crown of glory, and for a diadem of beauty, unto the residue of his people,", insight:"When man's proud crown falls, God Himself becomes the crown. The remnant's glory is not what they wear but who they belong to."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:6, to whom will He be a spirit of judgment?", options:["To him that sitteth in judgment","To the merchant in the market","To the singer in the choir","To the farmer in the field"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:6",verse:"And for a spirit of judgment to him that sitteth in judgment, and for strength to them that turn the battle to the gate.", insight:"God equips those who must judge with His own Spirit of judgment. Righteous decisions come from His wisdom, not human cleverness."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:7, who have erred through strong drink?", options:["The priest and the prophet","The king and the prince","The farmer and the shepherd","The mason and the carpenter"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:7",verse:"But they also have erred through wine, and through strong drink are out of the way; the priest and the prophet have erred through strong drink, they are swallowed up of wine, they are out of the way through strong drink; they err in vision, they stumble in judgment.", insight:"When even priests and prophets stumble through drink, the whole nation loses its way. Leaders' sins corrupt the vision of all."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:8, what fills all their tables?", options:["Vomit and filthiness","Bread and wine","Honey and oil","Milk and cheese"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:8",verse:"For all tables are full of vomit and filthiness, so that there is no place clean.", insight:"Drunkenness turns every table foul, leaving no place clean. Sin's excess always ends in shame, not satisfaction."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:9, whom shall He teach knowledge?", options:["Them that are weaned from the milk, and drawn from the breasts","The elders full of years","The princes of the court","The warriors of the army"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:9",verse:"Whom shall he teach knowledge? and whom shall he make to understand doctrine? them that are weaned from the milk, and drawn from the breasts.", insight:"God teaches those ready to move past milk to solid food. Spiritual growth requires leaving childish things behind."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:10, how must the teaching come?", options:["Precept upon precept, line upon line, here a little and there a little","All at once in a single day","Only through dreams in the night","Only by the voice of thunder"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:10",verse:"For precept must be upon precept, precept upon precept; line upon line, line upon line; here a little, and there a little:", insight:"God builds understanding gradually, line by line. Patience with His slow method is part of learning itself."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:11, with what will He speak to this people?", options:["With stammering lips and another tongue","With a voice of thunder","With the sound of harps","With silence and shadows"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:11",verse:"For with stammering lips and another tongue will he speak to this people.", insight:"Because they would not hear plain speech, God would speak through foreign tongues. Rejecting God's clear word brings harder lessons."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:12, what did they do when offered rest and refreshing?", options:["They would not hear","They sang for joy","They ran to the temple","They wept in repentance"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:12",verse:"To whom he said, This is the rest wherewith ye may cause the weary to rest; and this is the refreshing: yet they would not hear.", insight:"God offered rest to the weary and they refused to listen. The saddest rejection is turning away from the very relief we need."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:13, why was the word given to them precept upon precept?", options:["That they might go, and fall backward, and be broken, and snared, and taken","That they might grow wise and strong","That they might sing new songs","That they might build new altars"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:13",verse:"But the word of the LORD was unto them precept upon precept, precept upon precept; line upon line, line upon line; here a little, and there a little; that they might go, and fall backward, and be broken, and snared, and taken.", insight:"The same word that could have taught them becomes their snare. Light rejected turns into the very thing that trips us."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:14, who is told to hear the word of the LORD?", options:["Ye scornful men, that rule this people in Jerusalem","The little children at play","The strangers at the gate","The shepherds in the fields"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:14",verse:"Wherefore hear the word of the LORD, ye scornful men, that rule this people which is in Jerusalem.", insight:"God speaks directly to scornful rulers. Mockery of His word does not silence Him; it summons His warning."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:15, what did the rulers claim to have made a covenant with?", options:["Death, and with hell at agreement","The king of Assyria","The merchants of Egypt","The gods of the hills"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:15",verse:"Because ye have said, We have made a covenant with death, and with hell are we at agreement; when the overflowing scourge shall pass through, it shall not come unto us: for we have made lies our refuge, and under falsehood have we hid ourselves:", insight:"They bragged of a deal with death itself, hiding under lies. Every refuge built on falsehood will be swept away."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:16, what will God lay in Zion?", options:["A stone, a tried stone, a precious cornerstone, a sure foundation","A throne of cedar and gold","A wall of iron and brass","A gate of pearl and silver"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:16",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, I lay in Zion for a foundation a stone, a tried stone, a precious corner stone, a sure foundation: he that believeth shall not make haste.", insight:"God's answer to every crumbling refuge is one sure stone. Those who build their trust on Him will not need to flee in panic."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:18, what shall happen to their covenant with death?", options:["It shall be disannulled, and shall not stand","It shall be sealed forever","It shall be written in the temple","It shall be sung by the priests"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:18",verse:"And your covenant with death shall be disannulled, and your agreement with hell shall not stand; when the overflowing scourge shall pass through, then ye shall be trodden down by it.", insight:"Deals made with darkness do not hold when God's scourge passes through. Only His foundation stands when the storm comes."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:24, what question is asked about the plowman?", options:["Doth he plow all day to sow?","Doth he sleep till the harvest?","Doth he sell his oxen?","Doth he burn his field?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:24",verse:"Doth the plowman plow all day to sow? doth he open and break the clods of his ground?", insight:"The farmer does not plow forever; plowing has a purpose beyond itself. God's discipline, like the plow, is preparation for sowing, not endless punishment."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:27, how are the fitches beaten out?", options:["With a staff, and the cummin with a rod","With a cart wheel of iron","With the hooves of horses","With a millstone of rock"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:27",verse:"For the fitches are not threshed with a threshing instrument, neither is a cart wheel turned about upon the cummin; but the fitches are beaten out with a staff, and the cummin with a rod.", insight:"Delicate crops get the staff, not the cart wheel. God adjusts His threshing to what each of His people can bear."},
{q:"In Isaiah 28:29, in what is the LORD of hosts wonderful?", options:["In counsel, and excellent in working","In anger, and swift in judgment","In silence, and slow in answering","In hiding, and far in dwelling"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 28:29",verse:"This also cometh forth from the LORD of hosts, which is wonderful in counsel, and excellent in working.", insight:"The chapter that began with drunken folly ends with God's wonderful counsel. His wisdom in working far surpasses every human scheme."}
]},
{n:29, name:"Woe to Ariel", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 29:13",verse:"Wherefore the Lord said, Forasmuch as this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips do honour me, but have removed their heart far from me, and their fear toward me is taught by the precept of men:"}, facts:[
"Did you know Ariel is another name for Jerusalem? Verse 1 calls it the city where David dwelt, and God says He will distress it until there is heaviness and sorrow.",
"Did you know Isaiah 29:13 is quoted by Jesus in Matthew 15? He applied its warning, this people draw near me with their mouth, but their heart is far from me, to the religious leaders of His own day.",
"Did you know the potter and the clay return in this chapter? Verse 16 asks whether the thing framed can say to its maker, He had no understanding, reminding us that we cannot judge our Maker."
], questions:[
{q:"In Isaiah 29:1, what is Ariel called?", options:["The city where David dwelt","The city of the great king of Assyria","The city of the merchants","The city of the seven hills"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:1",verse:"Woe to Ariel, to Ariel, the city where David dwelt! add ye year to year; let them kill sacrifices.", insight:"Ariel is Jerusalem, David's own city. Judgment beginning at God's own city shows that privilege never excuses sin."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:2, what shall come upon Ariel?", options:["Heaviness and sorrow","Joy and singing","Gold and silver","Peace and plenty"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:2",verse:"Yet I will distress Ariel, and there shall be heaviness and sorrow: and it shall be unto me as Ariel.", insight:"God Himself will distress the city He loves. His chastening is heavy because His purpose, true repentance, is worth it."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:3, what will God do against Ariel?", options:["Camp against it, lay siege with a mount, and raise forts","Send rains to water its fields","Build its walls higher","Fill its storehouses with grain"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:3",verse:"And I will camp against thee round about, and will lay siege against thee with a mount, and I will raise forts against thee.", insight:"God can besiege His own city when it needs humbling. No walls keep out the Lord when He comes to correct."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:4, Ariel shall speak out of what?", options:["Out of the ground, and low out of the dust","From the top of the temple","From the king's palace","From the city gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:4",verse:"And thou shalt be brought down, and shalt speak out of the ground, and thy speech shall be low out of the dust, and thy voice shall be, as of one that hath a familiar spirit, out of the ground, and thy speech shall whisper out of the dust.", insight:"Pride brought low speaks from the dust. God reduces the lofty voice to a whisper so it learns humility."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:5, what shall the multitude of strangers be like?", options:["Like small dust, and as chaff that passeth away","Like mighty cedars of Lebanon","Like stars that never fade","Like rivers that never dry"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:5",verse:"Moreover the multitude of thy strangers shall be like small dust, and the multitude of the terrible ones shall be as chaff that passeth away: yea, it shall be at an instant suddenly.", insight:"The terrifying multitude is only dust and chaff to God. What frightens us most is weightless in His hand."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:6, with what shall Ariel be visited of the LORD of hosts?", options:["Thunder, earthquake, great noise, storm and tempest, and devouring fire","Gentle rain and soft dew","A cool breeze at evening","The singing of birds"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:6",verse:"Thou shalt be visited of the LORD of hosts with thunder, and with earthquake, and great noise, with storm and tempest, and the flame of devouring fire.", insight:"God's visitation can shake the earth itself. His power in judgment reminds us how small our strength is."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:7, what shall the nations that fight against Ariel be like?", options:["As a dream of a night vision","As an army that never retreats","As a wall that never falls","As a river that never dries"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:7",verse:"And the multitude of all the nations that fight against Ariel, even all that fight against her and her munition, and that distress her, shall be as a dream of a night vision.", insight:"The enemies' triumph will vanish like a dream at waking. Their victory is unreal; only God's purposes endure."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:8, what happens to the hungry man in the dream?", options:["He eateth, but awaketh with his soul empty","He findeth a full table","He sleepeth till the morning","He waketh satisfied and strong"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:8",verse:"It shall even be as when an hungry man dreameth, and, behold, he eateth; but he awaketh, and his soul is empty: or as when a thirsty man dreameth, and, behold, he drinketh; but he awaketh, and, behold, he is faint, and his soul hath appetite: so shall the multitude of all the nations be, that fight against mount Zion.", insight:"Chasing what cannot satisfy leaves the soul empty at waking. Only God fills the hunger that dreams cannot."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:9, they are drunken, but not with what?", options:["Wine, nor with strong drink","Water from the well","Milk from the flock","Oil from the press"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:9",verse:"Stay yourselves, and wonder; cry ye out, and cry: they are drunken, but not with wine; they stagger, but not with strong drink.", insight:"Their staggering comes from spiritual blindness, not wine. A nation can be drunk on its own delusions while perfectly sober."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:10, what spirit has the LORD poured out upon them?", options:["The spirit of deep sleep","The spirit of wisdom","The spirit of gladness","The spirit of strength"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:10",verse:"For the LORD hath poured out upon you the spirit of deep sleep, and hath closed your eyes: the prophets and your rulers, the seers hath he covered.", insight:"When people persistently close their eyes, God may seal them shut. Rejecting light long enough can end in deep sleep."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:11, what has the vision become like?", options:["The words of a book that is sealed","A song sung in the streets","A lamp set on a hill","A river running clear"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:11",verse:"And the vision of all is become unto you as the words of a book that is sealed, which men deliver to one that is learned, saying, Read this, I pray thee: and he saith, I cannot; for it is sealed:", insight:"God's word can become a sealed book to those who will not receive it. The same Scriptures that open heaven to the humble stay closed to the proud."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:12, what does the unlearned man say when handed the book?", options:["I am not learned","I will read it gladly","I know it already","I have no time"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:12",verse:"And the book is delivered to him that is not learned, saying, Read this, I pray thee: and he saith, I am not learned.", insight:"Both the learned and the unlearned find excuses. Learning is no substitute for a willing heart, and ignorance is no excuse either."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:13, what is wrong with this people?", options:["They honor God with lips, but their heart is far from Him","They never come to the temple","They refuse to offer sacrifices","They have forgotten His name"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:13",verse:"Wherefore the Lord said, Forasmuch as this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips do honour me, but have removed their heart far from me, and their fear toward me is taught by the precept of men:", insight:"God sees past the lips to the heart. Worship that is only mouth deep, taught by men's rules, is empty before Him."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:14, what shall perish?", options:["The wisdom of their wise men","The gold of their temple","The walls of their city","The ships of their merchants"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:14",verse:"Therefore, behold, I will proceed to do a marvellous work among this people, even a marvellous work and a wonder: for the wisdom of their wise men shall perish, and the understanding of their prudent men shall be hid.", insight:"God's marvellous work overturns human wisdom. The cleverest minds are confounded when He moves in wonder."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:15, woe is pronounced on those who do what?", options:["Seek deep to hide their counsel from the LORD","Build houses on the hills","Plant vineyards in the valleys","Trade with distant lands"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:15",verse:"Woe unto them that seek deep to hide their counsel from the LORD, and their works are in the dark, and they say, Who seeth us? and who knoweth us?", insight:"Hiding plans from God in the dark is folly. He sees in secret, and the question Who seeth us has one answer: He does."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:16, to what is man's questioning of God compared?", options:["The potter's clay saying to its maker, He made me not","A servant rebuking his master","A child correcting his father","A sheep leading its shepherd"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:16",verse:"Surely your turning of things upside down shall be esteemed as the potter’s clay: for shall the work say of him that made it, He made me not? or shall the thing framed say of him that framed it, He had no understanding?", insight:"The clay cannot judge the potter. Turning things upside down by questioning God's understanding is the height of pride."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:18, in that day who shall hear the words of the book?", options:["The deaf","The princes","The merchants","The soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:18",verse:"And in that day shall the deaf hear the words of the book, and the eyes of the blind shall see out of obscurity, and out of darkness.", insight:"God opens deaf ears to hear His word. What was sealed to the proud becomes clear to those He heals."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:20, who is brought to nought?", options:["The terrible one, and the scorner is consumed","The poor and the meek","The farmer and the shepherd","The child and the mother"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:20",verse:"For the terrible one is brought to nought, and the scorner is consumed, and all that watch for iniquity are cut off:", insight:"The terrible and the scornful will not last. God cuts off those who watch for iniquity and clears the ground for the meek."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:22, who redeemed Abraham?", options:["The LORD","The king of Salem","The elders of the city","The priests of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:22",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD, who redeemed Abraham, concerning the house of Jacob, Jacob shall not now be ashamed, neither shall his face now wax pale.", insight:"The God who redeemed Abraham still watches over Jacob's house. His past faithfulness is the ground of present hope."},
{q:"In Isaiah 29:24, who shall come to understanding?", options:["They that erred in spirit, and they that murmured shall learn doctrine","Only the priests and the Levites","Only those born in Jerusalem","Only the kings of the earth"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 29:24",verse:"They also that erred in spirit shall come to understanding, and they that murmured shall learn doctrine.", insight:"Even those who erred and murmured can learn. God's restoration reaches the confused and the complaining, not just the faithful."}
]},
{n:30, name:"In Returning and Rest", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 30:15",verse:"For thus saith the Lord GOD, the Holy One of Israel; In returning and rest shall ye be saved; in quietness and in confidence shall be your strength: and ye would not."}, facts:[
"Did you know Isaiah 30 warns against trusting Egypt instead of God? The people sent riches on donkeys and camels to buy Pharaoh's help, and God called it adding sin to sin.",
"Did you know verse 33 describes Tophet, a real valley near Jerusalem? Its fires became a picture of final judgment, kindled by the breath of the LORD like a stream of brimstone.",
"Did you know the promise of verse 26 pictures complete healing? When God binds up the breach of His people, even the moon will shine like the sun, and the sun seven times brighter."
], questions:[
{q:"In Isaiah 30:1, woe is pronounced on whom?", options:["The rebellious children","The faithful remnant","The singers of the temple","The keepers of the flocks"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:1",verse:"Woe to the rebellious children, saith the LORD, that take counsel, but not of me; and that cover with a covering, but not of my spirit, that they may add sin to sin:", insight:"They took counsel, but not of God, and covered themselves with a covering not of His Spirit. Planning without God only adds sin to sin."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:2, where did they walk to go down, without asking at God's mouth?", options:["Into Egypt","Into Assyria","Into Babylon","Into Moab"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:2",verse:"That walk to go down into Egypt, and have not asked at my mouth; to strengthen themselves in the strength of Pharaoh, and to trust in the shadow of Egypt!", insight:"They sought Pharaoh's strength without once asking God. Running to human power before seeking Him is the first step away from trust."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:3, what shall the strength of Pharaoh be to them?", options:["Your shame, and the trust in Egypt your confusion","Your glory and your honor","Your peace and your rest","Your wealth and your plenty"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:3",verse:"Therefore shall the strength of Pharaoh be your shame, and the trust in the shadow of Egypt your confusion.", insight:"The very help they trusted becomes their shame. Every hope placed in man instead of God ends in confusion."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:4, where were his princes?", options:["At Zoan, and his ambassadors at Hanes","At Damascus and at Tyre","At Jerusalem and at Hebron","At Babylon and at Nineveh"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:4",verse:"For his princes were at Zoan, and his ambassadors came to Hanes.", insight:"Their diplomats traveled far to courts that could not save them. Much effort spent on useless alliances is effort stolen from prayer."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:5, of what kind of people were they ashamed?", options:["A people that could not profit them","A people stronger than Pharaoh","A people rich in gold","A people skilled in war"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:5",verse:"They were all ashamed of a people that could not profit them, nor be an help nor profit, but a shame, and also a reproach.", insight:"Egypt could neither help nor profit, only shame. Trusting a broken reed pierces the hand that leans on it."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:6, what did they carry on asses and camels to a people that would not profit them?", options:["Their riches and their treasures","Their weapons of war","Their idols of gold","Their scrolls of the law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:6",verse:"The burden of the beasts of the south: into the land of trouble and anguish, from whence come the young and old lion, the viper and fiery flying serpent, they will carry their riches upon the shoulders of young asses, and their treasures upon the bunches of camels, to a people that shall not profit them.", insight:"They paid dearly, through lion and serpent country, for help that would never come. Misplaced trust is the most expensive bargain."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:7, what is Egypt's strength concerning this?", options:["To sit still, for they shall help in vain","To rise up in mighty power","To send chariots of iron","To guard the borders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:7",verse:"For the Egyptians shall help in vain, and to no purpose: therefore have I cried concerning this, Their strength is to sit still.", insight:"God names Egypt's true strength: sitting still. Boasted power that never moves is no strength at all."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:8, what was Isaiah told to do with the message?", options:["Write it in a table, and note it in a book, for the time to come","Speak it once and forget it","Whisper it only to the priests","Carve it on the city gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:8",verse:"Now go, write it before them in a table, and note it in a book, that it may be for the time to come for ever and ever:", insight:"God wanted this warning written down for generations. His rebukes are preserved so future hearts can learn from past folly."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:9, what kind of children are they?", options:["Rebellious, lying children that will not hear the law of the LORD","Obedient children of the covenant","Quiet children at rest","Hungry children at the table"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:9",verse:"That this is a rebellious people, lying children, children that will not hear the law of the LORD:", insight:"The root sin is refusing to hear God's law. Rebellion begins not with doing wrong but with stopping the ears."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:10, what did they ask the prophets to prophesy?", options:["Smooth things and deceits, not right things","Hard truths and warnings","The law of the LORD","Judgment on the nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:10",verse:"Which say to the seers, See not; and to the prophets, Prophesy not unto us right things, speak unto us smooth things, prophesy deceits:", insight:"They demanded comfortable lies from their prophets. Wanting only smooth words is how a people seals its own doom."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:11, what did they want to cease from before them?", options:["The Holy One of Israel","The king of Assyria","The tribute of Egypt","The sound of war"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:11",verse:"Get you out of the way, turn aside out of the path, cause the Holy One of Israel to cease from before us.", insight:"They asked to have God removed from their sight. The most dangerous prayer is the one that asks God to leave us alone."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:12, in what did they trust?", options:["Oppression and perverseness","The walls of Jerusalem","The swords of Egypt","The counsel of elders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:12",verse:"Wherefore thus saith the Holy One of Israel, Because ye despise this word, and trust in oppression and perverseness, and stay thereon:", insight:"They stayed themselves on oppression and twisted ways. Trusting in crookedness makes the fall certain, not the foundation sure."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:13, their iniquity shall be like what?", options:["A breach ready to fall, swelling out in a high wall","A seed planted in good ground","A lamp burning in the night","A river flowing to the sea"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:13",verse:"Therefore this iniquity shall be to you as a breach ready to fall, swelling out in a high wall, whose breaking cometh suddenly at an instant.", insight:"Sin hidden in a high wall swells until it bursts suddenly. What is covered up does not stay up; it comes down all at once."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:14, what shall not be found in the broken pieces?", options:["A sherd to take fire from the hearth, or to take water out of the pit","A single coin of silver","A fragment of the wall","A piece of the gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:14",verse:"And he shall break it as the breaking of the potters’ vessel that is broken in pieces; he shall not spare: so that there shall not be found in the bursting of it a sherd to take fire from the hearth, or to take water withal out of the pit.", insight:"The breaking will be so complete that not one useful shard remains. God's judgment leaves nothing to rebuild the old rebellion with."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:15, in what shall they be saved?", options:["In returning and rest","In horses and chariots","In gold and silver","In walls and towers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:15",verse:"For thus saith the Lord GOD, the Holy One of Israel; In returning and rest shall ye be saved; in quietness and in confidence shall be your strength: and ye would not.", insight:"Salvation comes not by striving but by returning and resting. Quiet confidence in God is true strength; they would not have it."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:16, what did they say instead?", options:["No, we will flee upon horses, and ride upon the swift","Yes, we will wait upon the LORD","No, we will hide in the caves","Yes, we will trust in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:16",verse:"But ye said, No; for we will flee upon horses; therefore shall ye flee: and, We will ride upon the swift; therefore shall they that pursue you be swift.", insight:"They chose speed over stillness, horses over hope. God answers that their swift flight will only be matched by swifter pursuers."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:17, how many shall flee at the rebuke of one?", options:["One thousand","One hundred","Ten thousand","Seven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:17",verse:"One thousand shall flee at the rebuke of one; at the rebuke of five shall ye flee: till ye be left as a beacon upon the top of a mountain, and as an ensign on an hill.", insight:"One rebuke scatters a thousand when God is not with them. Numbers mean nothing once His presence is withdrawn."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:18, why will the LORD wait?", options:["That He may be gracious unto you, and have mercy upon you","That He may gather His armies","That He may forget their sins","That He may build His temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:18",verse:"And therefore will the LORD wait, that he may be gracious unto you, and therefore will he be exalted, that he may have mercy upon you: for the LORD is a God of judgment: blessed are all they that wait for him.", insight:"God's waiting is mercy, not absence. He delays so that grace, not judgment, can have the last word for those who wait for Him."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:21, what shall their ears hear behind them?", options:["This is the way, walk ye in it","Turn back, the road is closed","Run swiftly, do not delay","Be silent, and do not move"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:21",verse:"And thine ears shall hear a word behind thee, saying, This is the way, walk ye in it, when ye turn to the right hand, and when ye turn to the left.", insight:"God guides even after we wander, speaking direction from behind. Whether we turn right or left, His voice shows the way."},
{q:"In Isaiah 30:26, how bright shall the sun be in the day God heals His people?", options:["Sevenfold, as the light of seven days","Twice as bright as before","As the stars at midnight","Dimmer than the moon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 30:26",verse:"Moreover the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, and the light of the sun shall be sevenfold, as the light of seven days, in the day that the LORD bindeth up the breach of his people, and healeth the stroke of their wound.", insight:"When God binds up His people's wounds, even creation shines brighter. His healing does not just repair; it glorifies."}
]}
]);
